exit 0
--- stdout
Usage: oikade COMMAND [ARGUMENTS] [FLAGS]

Write your home
  init      Start a home: a Go module that builds against Oikade.
  check     Find what's wrong with your home, as a deploy would.
  test      Run your home's tests.
  dev       Run your home against simulated devices.
  replay    Compare what your code would have done with what the host did.
  types     Write devices.go, with a Go handle for each of the host's devices.

Ship it
  deploy    Check and build your home, and run it on the host.
  rollback  Go back to the version the host ran before this one.

Watch and drive
  list      List what's on record, and how each is set up.
  status    Show what your home is doing now, and what's wrong.
  history   Show what your home did: each change, write and run, and why.
  tail      Follow your home's events as they happen.
  set       Set a capability, as a person would, through arbitration.
  pause     Pause a rule, so it lets go of what it holds until you resume it.
  resume    Resume a rule you paused.

Devices and pairing
  devices   Remove a device from the host, or every device of a plugin.
  setup     Give a plugin what it needs, such as a sign-in, on the host.
  matter    Show the Matter bridge, or open its window to pair a controller.

Host access
  login     Trust a host's network API, and use it from now on.
  client    Revoke a client of the host's network API.
  secret    Set or remove the secrets your home and its plugins read.
  backup    Back up the host's store, on the host, and print where it went.
  version   Print this oikade's version, and its host's when one answers.

oikade help COMMAND, or oikade COMMAND --help, says more of one.

Every command that talks to a host takes --data-dir DIR, for the host on this
machine, and --context NAME, which reaches a host you logged in to over its
network API. Without either, a command uses the context oikade login last saved,
or the host on this machine. OIKADE_TOKEN is the token of a context saved
without one. Every command takes --debug, or OIKADE_DEBUG=1, to show an error in
full.
--- stderr
